A||||7||Pretender||M.A.T sensor??
Z||000000||Pretender||07-24-99||05:40 PM||||can someone explain the purpose of it?.<br>
Z||000001||Cooter||07-24-99||09:05 PM||||MAT?? If it means manifold air temperature, it is the sensor that moniters the temp of the intake air flow. It helps the computer keep the mixture right by compensating for temp changes.
Z||000002||Pretender||07-25-99||02:06 PM||||is it like the mass-air sensor on othercars?...my friend just installed an 75mm mass-airflow sensor on his mustang..
Z||000003||Cooter||07-25-99||05:51 PM||||OH no! That is something completly different. In order to save a few bucks, Pontiac built the Fieros with "speed density" fuel injection. It uses several sensors (MAT, MAP, TPS, coolant temp etc.)to make an educated guess about how much fuel the engine needs at any particular time. A fuel injection system that uses a "mass air" sensor has a different sensor in the air stream that actually measures how much air is going into the engine. It usually has an air temp sensor and a heated wire that air passes over. By measuring the air temp and the amount of current it takes to keep the wire a certain temp. the computer reads actual airflow. These systems respond better to performance add on items because the computer can compensate for the increased air flow. I hope this helps.
Z||000004||Pretender||07-25-99||11:07 PM||||is there anyway to convert to a mass-air system instead of the super restrictive "ram-air" that we have.
Z||000005||Shaun41178||07-26-99||02:50 AM||||I have been told that if you place .47k resistors in the wiring leads in the MAT sensor you can pick up +2 hp.  This is what I was told by a guy with a 2.8 Camaro.  He said it was proven so I am guessing he had it tested or something.  I didn't ask any more questions about it.  I have the resistors but am unsure of how to install them.  Do I cut the wires and then splice in the resitors?  I think that is how it is done<p>Anyone else hear of this mod before?  I guess the resistors change the signal to the computer making the car run richer which produces a tad bit more power under the right conditions I am sure.  Just wanted some comments on this one as i think I am going to have it dyno tested.<p>
Z||000006||theogre||07-26-99||08:32 AM||||Conversion to mass air would require a complete rewiring job, a bunch of engine parts, and whole new ECM. The systems are completely diferant from what's on the V6 now. I'd think it would be more trouble than it's worth.
Z||000007||FieroMike||07-26-99||08:50 AM||||The resitor needs to complete the circuit so that the signal reads the desired resistence.  To install it you could stick each end of the resistor into the two terminals, or cut the wires, strip the ends and put one wire on each end.  It would only require 1 resistor.
